<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Appraisal glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Glossary</h1><p class="lede">40 terms you’ll meet in Indiana policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search 40 terms</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “depreciation” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"></div><p class="g-count" data-gcount aria-live="polite"></p><div class="az"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></div><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually replacement cost minus depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for increased living costs, such as temporary housing, while a home is uninhabitable after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It’s the question an appraisal panel decides.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition allowing either party to demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-B">Bad faith</dt><dd>A legal claim that an insurer breached its duty of good faith and fair dealing. It’s decided by courts, not appraisal panels.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-C">Coinsurance</dt><dd>A commercial policy provision that reduces payment if the property was insured below a required percentage of its value.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Collapse</dt><dd>A coverage provision, often narrow, for the abrupt falling down of a building or part of it from specified causes such as the weight of ice or snow.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage on a homeowners policy, for the house itself.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage, for detached garages, sheds, fences and similar structures.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property, or contents, coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property, limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss the policyholder pays before the policy pays.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value due to age, wear and obsolescence, used to calculate actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm with damaging straight-line winds, such as the June 2012 event that crossed Indiana.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Drip edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof edges to direct water into gutters; often missing from roof estimates.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-E">Endorsement</dt><dd>A written change to a policy that adds, removes or modifies coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Estimate review</dt><dd>A professional line-by-line comparison of the carrier’s estimate with the actual damage and current pricing.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-I">Ice and water shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering underlayment applied at eaves and valleys to resist leaks from ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at a roof’s edge that traps meltwater, which can back up under shingles and into the home.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Indiana Department of Insurance</dt><dd>The state agency that regulates insurers, agents and adjusters in Indiana and handles consumer complaints.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Indiana FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The residual market association that provides basic property insurance to owners unable to obtain it in the regular market.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-L">Like kind and quality</dt><dd>The standard for replacement materials that are similar in type and quality to the damaged property.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Whether replacement materials must reasonably match undamaged areas, a frequent issue with discontinued shingles and siding.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps taken after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping a roof or drying a wet basement.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added repair costs required by building codes or ordinances in effect at the time of repair.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charges for coordinating and supervising repairs, often 10% and 10%.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-P">Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which the insurer may request within a time set by the policy.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>The depreciation held back from an initial payment that a replacement cost policy pays after repairs are completed.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with like kind and quality, without deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and repairs needed, before pricing.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Square (roofing)</dt><dd>One hundred square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Steep charge</dt><dd>An estimate line item that compensates for slower work on steep roofs, commonly starting around 7/12 pitch.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>The policy provision setting how long the insured has to file a lawsuit, commonly two years in Indiana property policies.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an existing claim estimate, usually supported by documentation.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-T">Test square</dt><dd>A marked 10-by-10-foot area on a roof slope used to count hail impacts.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers can’t agree on.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Unfair claim settlement practices</dt><dd>Claim-handling conduct prohibited by Indiana insurance law and enforced by the Department of Insurance.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-W">Water backup endorsement</dt><dd>Optional coverage for water that backs up through sewers or drains or overflows from a sump, usually with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price property repairs by ZIP code.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sticky"><div class="side-box call-box"><p class="side-h">Talk it through</p><p>A free first conversation. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="call-num" href="tel:+18445371427">+1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/contact-us/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><p class="side-h">Resources</p><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-process-indiana/">Appraisal process guide</a></li><li><a href="/indiana-appraisal-questions/">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-appraisal-terms/" aria-current="page">Appraisal glossary</a></li><li><a href="/claim-dispute-guides/">Claim dispute guides</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
